report.txt
   1. NAME          extension .txt    (a convention; nothing enforces it)
   2. CONTENT       first bytes 89504e470d0a1a0a
      file(1) says something containing PNG
      file(1) says something containing image
   3. PERMISSION    mode -rw-r--r--  -- not executable
   4. WHAT OPENS IT the desktop asks a MIME database keyed on the NAME,
                    so it agrees with question 1 and not with 2

data.png
   1. NAME          extension .png    (a convention; nothing enforces it)
   2. CONTENT       first bytes 23212f62696e2f73
      file(1) says something containing shell
      file(1) says something containing script
      file(1) says something containing text
      file(1) says something containing ASCII
   3. PERMISSION    mode -rwxr-xr-x  -- the kernel will try to run it
   4. WHAT OPENS IT the desktop asks a MIME database keyed on the NAME,
                    so it agrees with question 1 and not with 2

THE FOUR QUESTIONS, AND WHY THEY DISAGREE WITHOUT ANY OF THEM BEING WRONG

   1. What is it CALLED?         the extension. A convention between
      humans; the filesystem stores a name and has no opinion.
   2. What is IN it?             magic bytes. file(1) reads a few and
      matches a table -- a guess, and it says so by ranking candidates.
   3. May it be EXECUTED?        a permission bit, which is about
      authority, not about content. An empty file can carry it.
   4. What OPENS it?             a desktop MIME association, usually
      resolved from the extension -- so it answers question 1 again.

report.txt is PNG content with a text name: 1 and 4 say text, 2 says PNG.
data.png is a script with an image name and the execute bit: 1 and 4 say
image, 2 says shell script, 3 says the kernel will try to run it -- and
the kernel is right, because ./data.png works and none of the other three
questions were consulted.

So 'what type is this file' has no answer on its own. It has four, from
four sources, and picking the wrong one is the whole of the security
advice on the subject: trust the CONTENT for what a thing is, and never
the name -- while remembering that content is a guess too.
